<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Tangier International Zone</h1><p class="st-meta">1924 CE – 1956 CE · capital Tangier</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Tangier under joint international administration from 1924 to 1956, a free port famous for banking, smuggling, espionage and expatriate writers.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Because of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier's</a> strategic position on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-gibraltar">Strait of Gibraltar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> agreed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1923 that the city and its surroundings would be neither French nor Spanish but internationalised. The statute took effect in 1924; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> joined in 1928, and other powers including Portugal, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Belgium</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> took part.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A committee of control of foreign consuls and an international legislative assembly governed, while the sultan was represented by a mendoub. Low taxes, free currency exchange and a free port attracted banks, traders, smugglers and spies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> occupied the zone during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, from 1940 to 1945, after which international rule was restored.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Moroccan independence, an international conference ended the special status in October 1956, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a> was reintegrated into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morocco">Kingdom of Morocco</a>, keeping a free-port charter for a few years.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tangier-international-zone.jpg" alt="Tangier International Zone" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Tangier_International_Zone.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1924 CE – 1956 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tangier</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam; Judaism; Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Spanish, French, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Morocco</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>International Zone of Tangier</dd></dl></aside></div>
